ng to go you know  you know maybe one day performing on  broadway or off broadway or  you know any theater would would be fine  but then it did it transferred to  broadway but when we finished the play i  was eight months pregnant with my  daughter  um  playing a virgin which  i don't know how successfully i did that  but but i was really gutted when i when  i  when i didn't get to to go off broadway  um but i had been offered a part in ron  howard's film and that was just much  more practical in terms of um  you know nurturing a a a new  relationship with with uh with with with  my with my newborn um and but i felt  like i just wasn't finished with with  the project but i you know at the same  time i knew that joe was  um  was  developing the project for the screen um  uh to make the film with erica uh pete's  wife who was  um writing the script for um for the  film  but you know that's a really long and  drawn out process and you know you never  know if films are going to get made or  if they're not and you know i knew i  knew that  you know the casting of pete would be  set in stone but but i'd you know i  didn't know that that uh that for sure  the you know the studio would approve my  casting what was it like to have the  familiarity of collaborating with peter  dinklage but the discovery of  collaborating with kelvin harrison i  what did you enjoy about sort of being  caught between the two of them as scene  partners i mean one of my favorite  scenes that i got to take part in was  the balcony scene where we all  were together in in one scene and it's  such a um  exciting scene for an actor um  uh there's there's such a fine balance  between you know what what roxanne is  aware of and what she isn't aware of and  and and i loved kind of that dynamic  between um with peter and and i i mean i  had to stop myself from laughing  um many times between like  during takes because i i just you know  they're both so dynamic and talented and  enigmatic and but it was kind of like  you know christian is  uh the puppet and and and um  and sierna was the puppeteer and you  know i  i loved kind of uh the way that calvin  his his um  his physicality that he used and that  scene and kind of he'd be talking you  know  he would be expressing himself even  though he wasn't the one saying the  words i i really love the kind of how  nuanced of a performance that um kelvin  gave in that scene  did you have a favorite song that you've  got to perform in this  um  yeah definitely i think it's i need more  uh because it's just a real it felt like  a real power ballad is also the song  that i was most afraid of  um because you know i had to reach such  great heights with with the with the  notes and  and and being that we  had to sing it live but also i think you  know it's one of the most liberating um  songs to perform  because uh you know i  i set out and i set out an intention to  not try to sound  good like i was like okay if i sound  ugly if i sound hideous if i don't hit a  note that's okay you know i i really i  wanted to free myself from those  expectations and i felt like maybe that  would make for a more interesting  performance of like rather  you know  you know  um and and and i and i loved it too  because you know i had all of this space  um to inhabit into like all of this  space to take up and i was like  i really enjoyed um and and relished in  taking up that much space  um  and it's not something  i feel like as an actor i've i've had  the chance to do  um and  i  i absolutely loved it  well i appreciate you talking to me  about it i love the strength and the  independence that this character has i  think she it just it's such a beautiful  character so thank you for talking about  her and the film  you
 

